# Role attestations

[Run the 1.0.0 release walkthrough in Livebook](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaselabs%2Fbounded_authority_protocol%2Fblob%2Fv1.0.0%2Fdocs%2Flivebooks%2Fbap-walkthrough.livemd).
For this focused journey, download this `.livemd` file and use **Open → Import from file** in Livebook.
The focused file is new since 1.0.0; the release launch above opens the released walkthrough.

Bind a public subject key to a role inside an attestor window, then refuse self-attestation, expiry, and cross-profile inputs.

Run every cell in order. Keys are generated only in memory; persisted outputs contain no keys,
signatures, credentials, or nonces. Verification leaves trust and execution policy with the host.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bounded_authority_protocol, "== 1.0.0"}])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

The cell returns `:ok`.

## Demonstration inputs

```elixir
notebook_value = (
  {issuer_public, issuer_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
  {holder_public, holder_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)

  {:ok, holder_thumbprint} =
    BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Jwk.public_key_thumbprint_raw(holder_public, %{})

  {byte_size(issuer_public), byte_size(holder_thumbprint)}

  alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{Grant, Operation}

  grant = %Grant{
    key_id: "issuer-1",
    issuer: "https://issuer.example",
    grant_id: "grant-2026-001",
    audiences: ["https://service.example"],
    issued_at: 1_800_000_000,
    not_before: 1_800_000_000,
    expires_at: 1_800_003_600,
    holder_thumbprint: holder_thumbprint,
    operations: [
      %Operation{
        name: "read_record",
        selectors: [
          {:equals, ["record", "region"], {:string, "us-east"}},
          {:one_of, ["record", "tier"], [{:string, "gold"}, {:string, "platinum"}]},
          :all
        ]
      }
    ]
  }

  {:ok, signing_input} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.grant_signing_input(grant, %{})
  issuer_signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, signing_input.message, [issuer_private, :ed25519])
  {:ok, grant_compact} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.assemble_compact(signing_input, issuer_signature)

  grant_compact

  now = 1_800_001_000
  :demo_ready
)
true = notebook_value === (
  :demo_ready
)
notebook_value
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:demo_ready
```

The cell returns `:demo_ready`.

## Produce and verify a role attestation

A role attestation is a standalone compact JWS (`typ: "ba+role-attestation"`), bound to no
grant, in which an attestor key binds a subject key to the `issuer` or `holder` role for a window
inside the attestor key's own validity. As with every sibling profile, selection is explicit: no
contract-major parses these bytes, and the profile parses none of theirs.

```elixir
notebook_value = (
  alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.RoleAttestation.V1, as: RoleAttestationV1
  alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.HistoricalPublicKey

  {attestor_public, attestor_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
  {subject_public, _subject_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)

  attestation = %RoleAttestationV1.RoleAttestation{
    attestor_key_id: "authority-attestor-1",
    jti: "urn:example:attestation:2026-001",
    key_id: "holder-key-1",
    public_key: subject_public,
    role: "issuer",
    nbf: now,
    exp: now + 3_600
  }

  {:ok, attestation_input} = RoleAttestationV1.attestation_signing_input(attestation, %{})

  attestation_signature =
    :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, attestation_input.message, [attestor_private, :ed25519])

  {:ok, attestation_compact} =
    RoleAttestationV1.assemble_compact(attestation_input, attestation_signature)

  expected_attestation = %RoleAttestationV1.ExpectedAttestation{
    attestor: %HistoricalPublicKey{
      key_id: "authority-attestor-1",
      public_key: attestor_public,
      valid_from: now - 600,
      valid_before: now + 7_200
    },
    subject_key_id: "holder-key-1",
    subject_public_key: subject_public,
    now: now + 60,
    bounds: %{}
  }

  {:ok, attestation_facts} =
    RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(attestation_compact, expected_attestation)

  Map.take(Map.from_struct(attestation_facts), [:attestor_key_id, :subject_key_id, :role, :trust])
)
true = notebook_value === (
  %{attestor_key_id: "authority-attestor-1", subject_key_id: "holder-key-1", role: "issuer", trust: :not_evaluated}
)
notebook_value
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{attestor_key_id: "authority-attestor-1", subject_key_id: "holder-key-1", role: "issuer", trust: :not_evaluated}
```

The cell returns `%{attestor_key_id: "authority-attestor-1", subject_key_id: "holder-key-1", role: "issuer", trust: :not_evaluated}`.
The facts say `trust: :not_evaluated` and carry no authorization marker at all.

## Role-attestation attacks fail closed

```elixir
notebook_value = (
  # A true self-attestation: the payload binds the attestor's own key id and key material, and
  # the expected subject matches it, so every binding check passes and only the structural
  # self-attestation rejection can fire.
  self_attestation = %{attestation | key_id: "authority-attestor-1", public_key: attestor_public}

  {:ok, self_input} = RoleAttestationV1.attestation_signing_input(self_attestation, %{})
  self_signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, self_input.message, [attestor_private, :ed25519])
  {:ok, self_compact} = RoleAttestationV1.assemble_compact(self_input, self_signature)

  %{
    self_attested:
      RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(self_compact, %{
        expected_attestation
        | subject_key_id: "authority-attestor-1",
          subject_public_key: attestor_public
      }),
    # now == exp: the acceptance window is the half-open [nbf, exp).
    now_at_exp:
      RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(attestation_compact, %{
        expected_attestation
        | now: now + 3_600
      }),
    # The attestation outlives the attestor key's own validity window.
    outliving_window:
      RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(attestation_compact, %{
        expected_attestation
        | attestor: %{expected_attestation.attestor | valid_before: now + 1_800}
      }),
    # Cross-profile, both directions.
    attestation_through_major_one:
      BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.decode_grant(attestation_compact, %{}),
    grant_through_attestation: RoleAttestationV1.decode_attestation(grant_compact, %{})
  }
)
true = notebook_value === (
  %{self_attested: {:error, :invalid}, now_at_exp: {:error, :invalid}, outliving_window: {:error, :invalid}, attestation_through_major_one: {:error, :invalid}, grant_through_attestation: {:error, :invalid}}
)
notebook_value
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{self_attested: {:error, :invalid}, now_at_exp: {:error, :invalid}, outliving_window: {:error, :invalid}, attestation_through_major_one: {:error, :invalid}, grant_through_attestation: {:error, :invalid}}
```

The cell returns `%{self_attested: {:error, :invalid}, now_at_exp: {:error, :invalid}, outliving_window: {:error, :invalid}, attestation_through_major_one: {:error, :invalid}, grant_through_attestation: {:error, :invalid}}`.

A role attestation is evidence of a binding, not an authorization: which attestor to trust,
which role a consumer requires, and replay reservation stay with the caller.
